.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt MOUNT_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount_minixfs
.Nd mount a MINIX file system image with FUSE
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Oo
.Fl w
.Op Fl u Cm always | sync | Ar seconds
.Oc
.Op Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Op Fl W Ar bits
.Op Ar FUSE options
.Ar image
.Ar mountpoint
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
mounts the MINIX file system in
.Ar image
on
.Ar mountpoint
through FUSE.
It supports V1, V2, V3 and Minix-vmd in either byte order;
see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.Pp
The mount is read-only unless
.Fl w
is given, and single-threaded: the options
.Fl s
and
.Fl o Ar ro ,
or
.Fl o Ar rw,default_permissions
with
.Fl w ,
are always given to FUSE.
Inode numbers, modes, link counts, owners, times and device numbers are
those of the image.
The image may be given as a relative path.
Other options, such as
.Fl f
to stay in the foreground or
.Fl o Ar allow_other ,
are passed on to FUSE.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl w
Enable creation, deletion, renaming and modification of files,
directories, links, pipes and devices.
The kernel checks permissions using the image's modes and owners.
New files belong to the caller.
They inherit the parent directory's group if it has set-group-ID or if
the caller's group ID does not fit the inode (above 255 in V1).
New directories also inherit the parent's set-group-ID bit.
Changes update mtime and ctime, but not atime.
.Pp
Files removed while open retain their data until closed.
On Linux and FreeBSD, libfuse uses temporary names consisting of
.Ql .fuse_hidden
and 16 hexadecimal digits; see
.Fl o
for their storage and
.Sx BUGS
for exceptions.
.Pp
The image is locked against other writers and marked unclean while
mounted for writing.
Unmounting restores the clean flag unless an error may have damaged the
image.
Unclean images are mounted read-only with a warning.
.It Fl u Cm always | sync | Ar seconds
Set when inode and zone bit maps are written:
.Bl -tag -width seconds -compact
.It Cm sync
At
.Xr fsync 2
and unmount (the default).
.It Cm always
After every change.
.It Ar seconds
At fsync and unmount, and on the first change after
.Ar seconds
have elapsed since the last bit map write.
.El
Data and other metadata are written as they change.
An interrupted mount leaves the image unclean; deferred bit maps may
not match the inodes.
Check and repair it with
.Nm fsck_minixfs Fl y .
.It Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Access an image containing the file system on one disk side.
See
.Xr minixfs 1
for the track layout.
.It Fl W Ar bits
Take the bit maps of a big-endian file system as words of
.Ar bits
bits, 8, 16, 32 or 64, instead of 16 for V1 and V2 with names of 14 and
8 for the rest; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.It Fl o Ar options
Pass a comma-separated option list to FUSE.
The following options are also accepted:
.Bl -tag -width hide=memory -compact
.It Cm rw , ro
Enable or disable writing, respectively.
.It Cm update= Ns Ar X
Equivalent to
.Fl u Ar X .
.It Cm tracks= Ns Ar X
Equivalent to
.Fl M Ar X .
.It Cm hide=disk
Store libfuse's temporary names in the directory (the default).
On file systems with 14-character names, these names are always stored
in memory because they do not fit.
.It Cm hide=memory
Store the temporary names in memory regardless of the name length.
.El
.El
.Pp
The file system is unmounted with
.Ql fusermount3 -u Ar mountpoint
on Linux, or with
.Xr umount 8
on the BSDs.
.Ss MINIX 3
On MINIX 3,
.Nm
is a service that
.Xr mount 8
starts, not a command.
It is built with
.Ql make fuse-minix
and installed as
.Pa /usr/pkg/service/minixfs ,
and
.Pa /etc/system.conf
is given an entry
.Ql service minixfs ,
a copy of that of ntfs-3g.
The image is mounted from a vnd device, with the options in
.Fl o :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# vndconfig vnd0 usr.img
# mount -t minixfs -o rw /dev/vnd0 /mnt
# umount /mnt
# vndconfig -u vnd0
.Ed
.Pp
Run by hand,
.Nm
shows how to mount and exits with 2.
A service has no standard error, so its warnings are not seen, and the
lock is on the vnd device, not on
.Ar image .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 when the file system has been unmounted, 1 if the image
cannot be opened, FUSE fails or the image cannot be written back, and 2
for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Look at an image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mount_minixfs usr.img /mnt
$ ls -l /mnt
$ fusermount3 -u /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
Copy a tree into an image, with the maps written as they change:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mount_minixfs -w -u always usr.img /mnt
$ cp -R src /mnt
$ fusermount3 -u /mnt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
.Sh BUGS
On MINIX 3, writing is not reliable, as libpuffs gets the new nodes
wrong: a symbolic link made through the mount loses its target, a pipe
is made with no valid type, and a character device is made a block
device.
.Pp
On
.Nx ,
a directory made in a set-group-ID directory takes its group but not
the set-group-ID bit, as librefuse sets the mode that the kernel gave
once the directory is made.
.Pp
On
.Nx
10.0 and later, looking up
.Ql \&..
in a directory right below the root fails, and the mount misbehaves
from then on, as it does for every file system served through
librefuse; see
.Pa docs/netbsd-refuse-dotdot.md
in the source.
.Pp
On
.Nx
and MINIX 3, librefuse passes on only the first of the opens of a file
that overlap, and says a file is closed at the first close.
A file removed while open is then kept until the unmount, not until it
is closed; and a file opened twice, closed once and then removed goes at
once, so that writing through the other descriptor changes a free inode,
or a file that took it.
.Pp
On
.Fx ,
.Xr ln 1
through the mount fails with EIO, although the link is made: libfuse
answers a hard link with a node number of its own for the new name, and
the fusefs of
.Fx
takes an answer with another node number than that of the file for a
failure.
.Pp
Where the names libfuse hides files under are kept in memory, a file
that is open and that the user renames to a name of that form is taken
for one that libfuse hides: the name goes at the unmount, and the file
with it unless it has another.
A file that is not open is renamed as to any other name.
.Pp
libfuse gives the link count of a file removed while open one too high
for a while after its mode, owner or times are changed through it: it
takes the name it hides the file under off the count it gives for
.Xr fstat 2 ,
but not off the one it gives back for the change, which the kernel keeps.
